  ' ============================================================
  ' BLACKBOARD NOTE
  ' ============================================================

  note as BlackboardNote
    **Blackboard — Shared problem-solving workspace (engine-owned)**

    The Blackboard is the GSM Sourcer realisation of the canonical
    Blackboard pattern's "shared memory". It is a strictly typed,
    panel-graph-aware data structure on which independent Knowledge
    Sources (KSs) post **Contributions** under the supervision of the
    Sourcer Controller. The Controller is **panel-graph-agnostic in
    semantics** — it knows only the declared DAG and each KS's Java
    `triggerPredicate(BlackboardView, SourcingRunContext)` method,
    not what any panel means.

    **One Java type for all panels.** A panel has no behaviour; it is
    metadata describing a typed region of the Blackboard. The engine
    therefore models every panel — engine-owned target panel and
    Pack-owned source panel alike — as one Java record
    `Panel` with fields `(name, kind, slots, dependsOnPanels)`,
    where `slots` maps each slot name to a `ContributionSlot`. A
    `ContributionSlot` is itself a composite child of the Panel: it
    carries the per-slot JSON Schema URI and `defaultResolver`
    (registration-time metadata) AND owns the live list of
    `Contribution`s posted into it during a Run (runtime state).
    There is no class hierarchy,
    no abstract `SourceAnalysisPanel`, no abstract
    `TargetDefinitionPanel`, no per-panel concrete subclass.
    Differentiation is by the `kind` field, not by Java type.
    Engine-vs-Pack ownership is derived from `kind`
    (TARGET_DEFINITION ⇒ engine; SOURCE_ANALYSIS ⇒ Pack); the
    registration validator enforces this and rejects Pack-declared
    target panels.

    **Boundary at registration, not at compile time.** Because Packs
    contribute panels as data (entries in `pack-manifest.panels[]`),
    the Sourcer Controller has zero compile-time dependency on Pack
    code for panel definitions. Pack-owned KS Java classes still load
    in sandboxed classloaders, but no Pack class extends a panel
    abstract — the engine's `PanelRegistry` accepts a `Panel` record
    regardless of who declared it, and enforces invariants at
    registration time (`kind = SOURCE_ANALYSIS` ⇒ name MUST be
    Pack-namespaced;
    `kind = TARGET_DEFINITION` ⇒ `owner` MUST be `ENGINE`; etc.).

    ====

    **Two panel kinds**

    1. **Source analysis panels** (`kind = SOURCE_ANALYSIS`,
       Pack-owned). Capture the source-side analysis of the artefact
       under sourcing — files, units, relations, interfaces. Each
       Pack ships its own panel instances in `pack-manifest.panels[]`.
       The engine ships none. The Repository Sourcing Pack (in
       `itip/`) currently declares four: `itip:SourceInventory`,
       `itip:SourceUnit`, `itip:SourceRelation`, `itip:SourceInterface`.
    2. **Target definition panels** (`kind = TARGET_DEFINITION`,
       engine-owned). Carry the GSM spine — Definition, Archetype,
       Statement, Granularity, Evidence, Confidence. They are sealed:
       tenants MAY add Pack-namespaced additive slots
       via `pack-manifest.extendsPanels[]` but MAY NOT redefine
       engine-defined slots or add new target panels at
       runtime.

    ====

    **Closed slot sets**

    Every `Panel` declares a closed `slots` map at
    Pack-registration time. Each entry binds a slot
    name (e.g. `Claim`, `Merge`, `Verdict`) to the JSON Schema URI
    that validates Contributions of that type. A `Contribution`
    belongs to exactly one `(panel, slot)` pair, with
    the slot name drawn from the panel's closed map.
    Adding a slot at Run time is rejected (invariant
    I-3 in the GSM Sourcing Blackboard skill). There is NO
    panel-level value schema; schemas live one-per-slot.

    ====

    **Conflict resolution and the resolved view**

    Each panel declares a default `ContributionResolver`. KSs may post
    multiple competing Contributions for the same `(panel,
    slot, identityKey)` triple; the resolver collapses them
    into a single resolved value visible via
    `BlackboardView.resolved(panel, identityKey)`. Resolvers:
    `highestConfidence`, `firstWriter`, `failOnConflict`,
    `userMediated`, `collectAll`, `compose` (schema-driven per-key
    merge of value objects — used by Statement so multiple
    StatementShapingKSs can populate disjoint archetype fields).
    `ConfidenceSynthesisKS`, `ReconciliationKS`, and ensemble KSs MUST
    use `BlackboardView.contributionsOf(panel, identityKey)` instead —
    `resolved` would already have collapsed the diversity those KSs
    need to reason over.

    ====

    **Engine run state distinct from the Blackboard**

    The Run input (operator intent, scope, focus, profile, resolver
    overrides, external tool bindings, run invariants) does NOT live on
    the Blackboard as a confidence-1.0 hypothesis (a deliberate
    departure from Hearsay-II). It lives in `SourcingRunContext` — a
    **frozen run-scoped engine value, distinct from the Blackboard** —
    accessed via `controlContext.runContext()`.

    ====

    **Panel DAG (engine-validated at Pack registration)**

    ```
    itip:SourceInventory  ->  itip:SourceUnit
    itip:SourceUnit       ->  itip:SourceInterface
    itip:SourceUnit       ->  itip:SourceRelation
    {itip:SourceInterface, itip:SourceUnit, itip:SourceRelation}
                          ->  Definition  <<bridge>>
    Definition            ->  Archetype
    Archetype             ->  Statement
    Statement             ->  Granularity
    Statement             ->  Evidence
    Evidence              ->  Confidence
    ```

    A KS's `readsPanels` MUST be a subset of the transitive ancestors
    of its `writesPanel` in this DAG. Cross-DAG reads and cycles are
    rejected at Pack registration.
  end note

  ' ============================================================
  ' PANEL — single Java record (no hierarchy)
  ' ============================================================

  class Panel <<engine,record>> {
    +name: String
    +kind: PanelKind
    +slots: Map<String, ContributionSlot>
    +dependsOnPanels: List<String>
  }

  class ContributionSlot <<engine,record>> {
    +name: String
    +schemaUri: URI
    +defaultResolver: ContributionResolver
    +contributions: List<Contribution>
  }

  ' Composition: Panel is a composite of ContributionSlots,
  ' and each ContributionSlot owns its Contributions.
  Panel "1" *-- "1..*" ContributionSlot : composes >
  ContributionSlot "1" *-- "0..*" Contribution : holds >

  note bottom of Panel
    **Panel** — Typed region of the Blackboard, modelled as a
    composite of `ContributionSlot`s.

    **Concept Semantics**:

    - A Panel is a *composite container*. Its `slots` map binds each
      slot name to a `ContributionSlot` instance, and each
      `ContributionSlot` in turn owns the live list of
      `Contribution`s posted into it during a Run. The composition
      arrows on the diagram are normative: `Panel →*→ ContributionSlot
      →*→ Contribution`.
    - The `(name, kind, dependsOnPanels)` triple plus the slot-map
      shape (slot names + each slot's `schemaUri` and
      `defaultResolver`) is the panel's static **registration**
      metadata. The `contributions` list inside each slot is the
      panel's **runtime** state, populated as a Run progresses.
    - Both engine-owned (target) and Pack-owned (source) panels are
      instances of THIS record. The engine ships no panel base class
      and no per-panel subclass. Differentiation is by `kind`
      (SOURCE_ANALYSIS vs TARGET_DEFINITION) as data; ownership
      (engine vs Pack) is derived from `kind`.
    - The slot map is CLOSED at Pack-registration time. One slot =
      one entry in `slots`; one Contribution belongs to exactly one
      `(panel, slot)` pair and is held in that slot's
      `contributions` list.
    - The Panel knows its place in the engine-validated panel DAG
      via `dependsOnPanels`. The DAG is validated for acyclicity and
      cross-DAG-read containment at Pack registration.

    **Field Semantics**:

    - `name`: panel name. MUST be Pack-namespaced when
      `kind = SOURCE_ANALYSIS` (e.g. `itip:SourceUnit`); engine-owned
      target panels use unqualified GSM-spine names (Definition,
      Archetype, Statement, Granularity, Evidence, Confidence).
    - `kind`: SOURCE_ANALYSIS or TARGET_DEFINITION. Determines the
      identity-key contract (target panels REQUIRE
      `targetIdentityKey` on every Contribution; source panels MAY
      omit it), the DAG-bridging contract (only source panels may
      bridge to a target panel), and engine-vs-Pack ownership
      (TARGET_DEFINITION ⇒ engine-owned and sealed;
      SOURCE_ANALYSIS ⇒ Pack-owned).
    - `slots`: closed map binding each accepted slot name to a
      `ContributionSlot` (a composite child of this Panel) carrying:
      - `name`: the slot's name (same as the map key).
      - `schemaUri`: versioned `gsmarc://` URI of the JSON Schema
        that every `Contribution.value` posted into this slot MUST
        satisfy.
      - `defaultResolver`: the `ContributionResolver` strategy used
        to fold competing Contributions held by THIS slot. RunConfig
        may override per `(panel, slot)`.
      - `contributions`: live list of `Contribution`s posted into
        this slot during the current Run.
      Engine-defined entries on engine-owned panels are sealed.
      Packs MAY add Pack-namespaced additive entries via
      `pack-manifest.extendsPanels[]`. Schema URIs resolve to
      resources bundled in the engine or Pack JAR at
      `/META-INF/gsmarc/<URI-tail>`.
    - `dependsOnPanels`: panel-DAG edges. Engine-owned panel names
      are allowed for upward bridges from source to target.

    **Validation Rules (engine-enforced at registration)**:

    - `name` MUST be unique within a Run.
    - `kind = SOURCE_ANALYSIS` ⇒ `name` MUST be Pack-namespaced.
    - `kind = TARGET_DEFINITION` ⇒ panel MUST be declared by the
      engine itself (Pack manifests cannot declare TARGET_DEFINITION
      panels in `panels[]`; only additive slot entries
      are allowed via `extendsPanels[]`).
    - `dependsOnPanels` MUST NOT introduce a cycle in the DAG.
    - A bridging edge (source ⇒ target) is permitted only when the
      source panel has `kind = SOURCE_ANALYSIS` and the target panel
      has `kind = TARGET_DEFINITION`.
    - A Contribution accepted into this Panel MUST carry a
      `slot` that is a key in `slots`; the
      Contribution's `value` MUST validate against the bound schema.
    - A Contribution with `determinism = NON_DETERMINISTIC` MUST
      carry `cacheKey` (I-4).
    - A Contribution with `kind = USER_OVERRIDE` MUST carry
      `checkpointId` (I-11).

    **Derivation Rules**:

    - For ENGINE-owned target panels: instances are engine constants
      (see `Panel ontology` below) instantiated by the Blackboard
      Builder substep.
    - For PACK-owned source panels: instances are constructed from
      `pack-manifest.panels[]` entries by the Blackboard Builder.

    **Decision Invariants — when to introduce a new Panel vs a new
    slot** (engine-validated at Pack registration where
    possible; complement to runtime invariants I-1..I-15 in the GSM
    Sourcing Blackboard skill)

    Provenance tag legend:
      [HSII]  inherited directly from the canonical Hearsay-II /
              Blackboard pattern
      [GSM-S] GSM Sourcer engineering choice (NOT canonical); kept
              honest so the design tradeoff stays visible
      [OVL]   GSM Sourcer governance overlay (Checkpoint / Override
              Cascader); has no counterpart in the canonical pattern

    - **P-1 [HSII] Producer/consumer separation**: a new Panel is
      justified IFF at least one consumer reads this typed region
      while WRITING somewhere else. The consumer MAY be another KS
      OR an engine stage (e.g. the Validation/Registration
      Pipeline reads `Confidence` even though no KS does). If no
      consumer exists distinct from the producer KS(s), the
      candidate region is a slot of an existing panel,
      not a new panel.
    - **P-2 [HSII] Distinct DAG node**: a new Panel MUST occupy a
      distinct node in the panel DAG. If the candidate would have
      the same `dependsOnPanels` set AND the same downstream
      readers as an existing panel, it is a slot of
      that panel.
    - **P-3 [GSM-S] Resolver granularity (DISSOLVED 2026-04)**:
      previously, GSM Sourcer attached the resolver at panel
      granularity, which forced any aspect needing a different
      resolution strategy to become its own panel — a structural
      tension with P-2 (same-DAG-position aspects must share the
      panel). The resolver is now attached PER
      `(panel, slot)` via
      `slots[ct].defaultResolver`, with RunConfig
      override key `resolvers."<Panel>.<Slot>"`. P-3 no
      longer constrains panel-vs-type decisions. Retained as a
      named ID for traceability; effectively a no-op rule.
    - **P-4 [OVL] Override cascade boundary**: a USER_OVERRIDE on
      any slot of a panel cascades to the panel's
      DAG-downstream consumers under one common cascade rule (per
      I-11). This is a GSM Sourcer governance-overlay rule with no
      Hearsay-II counterpart. Consequence: if the candidate aspect
      must cascade differently than the rest of the panel, it MUST
      be its own panel.
    - **P-5 [HSII] Independent fillability**: slots of
      the same panel MUST be fillable by independent KSs in any
      order. If two aspects MUST be co-asserted by the same KS in
      one Contribution (a transactional invariant), they are the
      same slot, not two.

    Negative form (anti-patterns the registration validator does NOT
    catch but reviewers MUST):

    - **Type-as-panel** (P-1/P-2 violation): splitting one logical
      level into N panels with identical DAG positions, fragmenting
      the resolved view and forcing readers to do N-way joins.
    - **Panel-as-type** (P-1 violation): folding a distinct
      abstraction level into a slot of an existing
      panel, hiding it from the DAG and denying its readers the
      ability to subscribe independently. (Pre-2026-04 this was
      also a P-3 violation; with P-3 dissolved, only P-1 still
      catches it.)
  end note

  enum PanelKind {
    SOURCE_ANALYSIS
    TARGET_DEFINITION
  }

  enum ContributionResolver {
    highestConfidence
    firstWriter
    failOnConflict
    userMediated
    collectAll
    compose
  }

  enum DefinitionSubjectKind {
    STRUCTURE
    MECHANISM
    EFFECTOR
    RECEPTOR
    INTERACTION
    ARCHETYPE
    DIRECTIVE
    NORM
  }

  enum GranularityVerdict {
    keep
    drop
    splitInto
    mergeWith
  }

  ' ============================================================
  ' PANEL ONTOLOGY — engine-owned target spine (instances of Panel)
  ' ============================================================

  package "Panel ontology — engine-owned target spine\n(instances of Panel)" as TGT {

    object "Definition : Panel" as DefinitionPanel {
      name = "Definition"
      kind = TARGET_DEFINITION
      slots[Claim].schemaUri = gsmarc://gsm-sourcer/panels/Definition/Claim/v1
      slots[Claim].defaultResolver = highestConfidence
      slots[Merge].schemaUri = gsmarc://gsm-sourcer/panels/Definition/Merge/v1
      slots[Merge].defaultResolver = collectAll
      dependsOnPanels = []
    }

    note bottom of DefinitionPanel
      **Definition** — Identity-binding panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Decides WHICH source items collectively define ONE GSM
        subject (Structure, Mechanism, Effector, Receptor,
        Interaction, Archetype, Directive, Norm). Join key for every
        downstream target panel.
      - Two slots (closed):
        - `Claim` — an `IdentificationKS` asserts an identity for a
          subject based on source-panel evidence. `value` carries
          `subjectKind` (DefinitionSubjectKind), `identityKey`
          (mints the join key), and `supportingSourceIds`.
        - `Merge` — a `ReconciliationKS` asserts that two prior
          identities describe the same subject. `value` carries
          `supersedesIdentityKeys` (non-empty) and a surviving
          `targetIdentityKey`.
      - Default resolvers (per slot): `Claim` →
        `highestConfidence`, `Merge` → `collectAll`. Equally-
        confident
        `Claim`s on the same surface key escalate to a Checkpoint via
        a `failOnConflict` / `userMediated` RunConfig override.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - `Claim.value.identityKey` is the engine's join key for
        downstream panels; required.
      - `Merge.value.supersedesIdentityKeys[]` MUST resolve to prior
        `Claim` Contributions on the Blackboard.

      **Merge precedence rule (Definition.Merge vs Granularity.mergeWith)**:

      - `Definition.Merge` is identity-time merging (pre-Statement),
        based on source-side signals only.
      - `Granularity.mergeWith` is post-statement consolidation,
        informed by the populated Statement.
      - When both apply to the same identity, `Granularity.mergeWith`
        wins (more informed). The Override Cascader supersedes the
        earlier `Definition.Merge` and re-fires downstream triggers
        per I-11 cascade scope.
    end note

    object "Archetype : Panel" as ArchetypePanel {
      name = "Archetype"
      kind = TARGET_DEFINITION
      slots[Application].schemaUri = gsmarc://gsm-sourcer/panels/Archetype/Application/v1
      slots[Application].defaultResolver = collectAll
      dependsOnPanels = [Definition]
    }

    note bottom of ArchetypePanel
      **Archetype** — Archetype-application panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Captures which GSM Archetype(s) apply to a given Definition.
        One slot (closed):
        - `Application` — an `ArchetypeKS` posts one archetype
          reference per Contribution. `value` carries `archetypeRef`
          (versioned `gsmarc://` URI of the GSM Archetype Definition).
      - Default resolver (`Application`): `collectAll` (union over Applications). The
        Pipeline reads the union as the resolved archetype set for
        the Definition.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - `Application.value.archetypeRef` MUST resolve to a registered
        Archetype.
      - The asserted Archetype's structural-base (allOf chain) MUST
        be compatible with the resolved
        `Definition.Claim.subjectKind` for the same identity.
    end note

    object "Statement : Panel" as StatementPanel {
      name = "Statement"
      kind = TARGET_DEFINITION
      slots[Draft].schemaUri = gsmarc://gsm-sourcer/panels/Statement/Draft/v1
      slots[Draft].defaultResolver = compose
      dependsOnPanels = [Archetype]
    }

    note bottom of StatementPanel
      **Statement** — Statement-shaping panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Holds populated GSM statement payloads conforming to the
        resolved Archetype's JSON Schema. One slot
        (closed):
        - `Draft` — a `StatementShapingKS` posts a partial or full
          statement payload. `value` carries `statement` (the
          archetype-typed payload), `completeness` (`partial` |
          `full`), and `shapingMethod` (free-form classifier such as
          `structural-extraction`, `llm-completion`).
      - Default resolver (`Draft`): `compose`. Multiple StatementShapingKSs may
        post Contributions populating disjoint archetype fields; the
        engine's schema-driven per-key merge assembles the union into
        the resolved Statement. This is the panel-level realisation
        of cooperative shaping.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - `Draft.value.statement` MUST validate against the resolved
        Archetype schema for the targeted Definition (incremental
        schema check at post time).
      - LLM-driven Contributions MUST carry `determinism =
        NON_DETERMINISTIC` and a `cacheKey` (I-4).
      - The Pipeline's Validation stage rejects compositions that
        remain `partial` after resolution.
    end note

    object "Granularity : Panel" as GranularityPanel {
      name = "Granularity"
      kind = TARGET_DEFINITION
      slots[Verdict].schemaUri = gsmarc://gsm-sourcer/panels/Granularity/Verdict/v1
      slots[Verdict].defaultResolver = highestConfidence
      dependsOnPanels = [Statement]
    }

    note bottom of GranularityPanel
      **Granularity** — Post-statement consolidation panel of the
      GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Holds per-Definition consolidation verdicts AFTER Statement
        shaping. One slot (closed):
        - `Verdict` — a `GranularityKS` posts one verdict per
          identity. `value.verdict` is one of `keep`, `drop`,
          `splitInto`, `mergeWith` (see `GranularityVerdict` enum).
          `mergeWith` carries `mergeWithIdentityKey`; `splitInto`
          carries non-empty `splitIntoIdentityKeys[]`.
      - Distinct from `Definition.Merge`: Granularity runs AFTER
        Statement shaping, so its `mergeWith` verdict is informed by
        the populated GSM statement and TAKES PRECEDENCE over any
        earlier identity-time `Definition.Merge` for the same
        identity (Override Cascader supersedes the earlier `Merge`).

      **Validation Rules**:

      - `mergeWith` ⇒ `mergeWithIdentityKey` MUST resolve to a
        Definition on the Blackboard.
      - `splitInto` ⇒ `splitIntoIdentityKeys[]` non-empty; each new
        identity yields a fresh Definition + downstream-target
        re-fire.
    end note

    object "Evidence : Panel" as EvidencePanel {
      name = "Evidence"
      kind = TARGET_DEFINITION
      slots[Signal].schemaUri = gsmarc://gsm-sourcer/panels/Evidence/Signal/v1
      slots[Signal].defaultResolver = collectAll
      dependsOnPanels = [Statement]
    }

    note bottom of EvidencePanel
      **Evidence** — Confidence-feeding panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Aggregates positive or negative signals about a Definition's
        Statement and surrounding context. One slot
        (closed):
        - `Signal` — an `EvidenceCollectionKS` posts one weighted
          signal per Contribution. `value.weight ∈ [-1, 1]`;
          `value.evidenceKind` is a free-form classifier
          (`test-pass`, `test-fail`, `interface-coherence`,
          `relation-density`, ...). Additive — every Signal counts.
      - Default resolver (`Signal`): `collectAll`. The resolved view is the
        full list of Signals; `ConfidenceSynthesisKS` reads the list
        via `BlackboardView.contributionsOf(...)` (NOT `resolved`)
        to compute a Confidence Score.
    end note

    object "Confidence : Panel" as ConfidencePanel {
      name = "Confidence"
      kind = TARGET_DEFINITION
      slots[Score].schemaUri = gsmarc://gsm-sourcer/panels/Confidence/Score/v1
      slots[Score].defaultResolver = highestConfidence
      dependsOnPanels = [Evidence]
    }

    note bottom of ConfidencePanel
      **Confidence** — Confidence-synthesis panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Holds the synthesised confidence score per Definition (and
        optionally per downstream target panel: Definition,
        Archetype, Statement, Granularity). One slot
        (closed):
        - `Score` — a `ConfidenceSynthesisKS` posts one Score
          Contribution per identity. `value.score ∈ [0, 1]`;
          `value.targetPanel` ∈ {Definition, Archetype, Statement,
          Granularity}.
      - Default resolver (`Score`): `highestConfidence`. The Pipeline's
        Validation stage MAY require a minimum Score per identity
        before producing a `ValidatedAscriptionDraft`.
      - `BlackboardView` exposes a Java accessor
        `confidenceBelow(identity, threshold)` so a KS's
        `triggerPredicate` can express revision triggers — a low
        Score can re-fire upstream KSs even when their nominal
        upstream contribution is already resolved.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - `targetPanel` MUST resolve to a registered engine-owned
        target panel other than `Evidence` and other than
        `Confidence` itself.
    end note
  }

